1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,280
On day twelve, all that stands between a
bus and a kid is ten seconds.

2
00:00:08,833 --> 00:00:12,753
But on day one, you just wake up and
discover you can pause time.

3
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:17,533
The alarm's frozen mid-ring. Your cat's
stuck halfway off the bed.

4
00:00:18,333 --> 00:00:21,693
So what if you could pause time for ten
seconds a day?

5
00:00:23,433 --> 00:00:29,213
Ten seconds. Once a day. Everyone and
everything stops. Except you.

6
00:00:29,967 --> 00:00:34,047
When the ten run out, the world starts
again, and nobody knows.

7
00:00:34,833 --> 00:00:37,153
And unused seconds vanish at midnight.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:41,520
So we cheat. Air moves for you. You can
see.

9
00:00:41,540 --> 00:00:46,580
And anything you touch moves with you,
then stops dead when you let go.

10
00:00:47,833 --> 00:00:49,853
So, what's your first pause for?

11
00:00:50,533 --> 00:00:52,413
Something selfish. Obviously.

12
00:00:53,433 --> 00:00:59,113
A phone falls a metre in about 0.45
seconds. Usually, you just watch it go.

13
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:00,900
Not today.

14
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:03,893
You stroll over. There's no rush.

15
00:01:04,473 --> 00:01:08,953
At an ordinary walking pace, ten seconds
covers about 10 to 14 metres.

16
00:01:09,833 --> 00:01:10,833
Not a crack.

17
00:01:12,033 --> 00:01:15,713
Your boss spots you in the hallway. Stop
and chat, or vanish?

18
00:01:16,533 --> 00:01:17,533
Vanish.

19
00:01:19,167 --> 00:01:24,207
The last samosa. A colleague's hand is
already reaching. Do you let it go?

20
00:01:25,633 --> 00:01:29,733
Of course not. You're not a saint. You're
a person with ten seconds.

21
00:01:30,633 --> 00:01:34,753
Your mother's chappal, mid-air, aimed at
your head? You step aside.

22
00:01:35,393 --> 00:01:36,993
She never works out how she missed.

23
00:01:38,433 --> 00:01:42,493
Have you ever glanced at a clock and the
second hand seemed to stick for a moment?

24
00:01:43,233 --> 00:01:46,413
That's real. Scientists call it
chronostasis.

25
00:01:47,233 --> 00:01:51,893
Your brain has been editing time for you
all your life. Now you've got the remote.

26
00:01:53,133 --> 00:01:55,893
Then comes day twelve. You're walking
home.

27
00:01:56,633 --> 00:01:58,013
A bus runs a red light.

28
00:01:58,833 --> 00:01:59,833
There's a kid in the road.

29
00:02:00,667 --> 00:02:07,047
A car at 48 kilometres an hour needs about
23 metres to stop. And this is a bus.

30
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:09,720
Do you shout? Do you run?

31
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:12,500
Neither works. Not in time.

32
00:02:13,267 --> 00:02:14,267
You hit pause.

33
00:02:15,300 --> 00:02:16,300
You run.

34
00:02:16,833 --> 00:02:19,753
Nine. Eight. Seven.

35
00:02:20,600 --> 00:02:23,380
Six. Five. Four.

36
00:02:24,667 --> 00:02:27,147
Three. Two. One.

37
00:02:28,133 --> 00:02:32,913
The bus screams through an empty crossing.
The ball doesn't make it. The kid does.

38
00:02:33,667 --> 00:02:38,887
The kid's mother runs over, shaking. She
thanks the bus driver. Nobody thanks you.

39
00:02:39,287 --> 00:02:40,287
Nobody knows to.

40
00:02:43,100 --> 00:02:46,620
You saved a life with ten seconds. That
feels free.

41
00:02:47,467 --> 00:02:48,467
It is not free.

42
00:02:49,200 --> 00:02:52,460
Your body keeps ageing through every
pause.

43
00:02:52,480 --> 00:02:55,020
Ten seconds a day that nobody else lives.

44
00:02:55,733 --> 00:03:00,373
And now you know what ten seconds can do.
So you can't stop watching.

45
00:03:01,167 --> 00:03:03,747
One morning you're late, and the bus doors
are closing.

46
00:03:04,267 --> 00:03:05,727
Catch it, or keep your seconds?

47
00:03:06,567 --> 00:03:07,567
Just this once.

48
00:03:09,267 --> 00:03:13,287
That evening, your mother misses a step on
the stairs. You snap your fingers.

49
00:03:13,307 --> 00:03:14,467
Nothing.

50
00:03:19,433 --> 00:03:22,073
She's all right. Bruised. You're not.

51
00:03:22,967 --> 00:03:27,627
That night, you lie awake doing the maths
of every second you've ever wasted.

52
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,760
You almost never spend them on yourself
again.

53
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:36,640
Some days nothing happens. So at midnight
you spend them on nothing at all.

54
00:03:38,967 --> 00:03:42,287
Ten seconds of a city holding its breath,
just for you.

55
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:48,260
Except, across the street, a curtain
moves. In a window that should be frozen.

56
00:03:49,633 --> 00:03:53,593
The curtain never moves again. But every
midnight, you check.

57
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:57,940
Catch the six that wins your lane's
cricket match? That, you do.

58
00:03:58,360 --> 00:03:59,360
Just the once.

59
00:04:00,767 --> 00:04:05,807
Ten seconds is too small for greed. It's
exactly the right size for an accident.

60
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:10,440
You become the reason small disasters
don't happen.

61
00:04:11,200 --> 00:04:14,140
Your mother asks you to stop waiting at
the bottom of the stairs.

62
00:04:14,967 --> 00:04:16,727
Promise her, or keep watching?

63
00:04:17,567 --> 00:04:18,887
You stay on the bottom step.

64
00:04:23,233 --> 00:04:26,313
Forty years in, you read a street like a
weather map.

65
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:31,653
You see the accident before it happens.
Most days, you don't even need the pause.

66
00:04:32,467 --> 00:04:35,047
Your body lives every one of those pauses.

67
00:04:35,647 --> 00:04:38,607
Over fifty years, they add up to about two
days.

68
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,240
That's how much older you are than
everyone you grew up with.

69
00:04:42,260 --> 00:04:43,820
You'd barely notice.

70
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:46,440
The bigger cost is the watching.

71
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:52,240
A whole life spent ten seconds at a time,
on strangers who'll never know your name.

72
00:04:52,967 --> 00:04:54,607
And then you notice something.

73
00:04:58,067 --> 00:04:59,547
Someone else is still moving.

74
00:05:00,400 --> 00:05:01,800
And they're looking right at you.

75
00:05:02,500 --> 00:05:03,500
Somebody knows.

76
00:05:05,200 --> 00:05:08,280
So what would you spend your ten seconds
on? Tell us below.

77
00:05:08,300 --> 00:05:11,160
The best what-ifs become videos.
